    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 13).**\n\n**Symptom.** `~/.cache/hypergumbo` grows without bounds. Concrete observation on this dev machine:\n\n```\n$ hypergumbo cache-status\nCache directory: /home/jgstern_agent/.cache/hypergumbo\nEntries: 4\nTotal size: 7.9 GB\nAge range: 0-2 days\n\n$ du -sh ~/.cache/hypergumbo/*\n7.9G  /home/jgstern_agent/.cache/hypergumbo/473332f2ea511777   ← hypergumbo repo\n104K  /home/jgstern_agent/.cache/hypergumbo/9f18923999f7a303\n12K   /home/jgstern_agent/.cache/hypergumbo/e9671acd244849c5\n148K  /home/jgstern_agent/.cache/hypergumbo/tracker\n\n$ ls ~/.cache/hypergumbo/473332f2ea511777/results/ | wc -l\n76\n```\n\n**One repo's \"results\" cache contains 76 sub-entries totaling 7.9 GB**, accumulated over 3 days (May 14-16). At ~104 MB per behavior-map result × 76 entries = ~7.9 GB. Growth rate: ~25 entries/day × ~104 MB ≈ **2.6 GB/day**.\n\n**Three problems.**\n\n1. **No automatic eviction.** Every `hypergumbo run` writes a new cache entry indexed by config_fingerprint. Nothing ever evicts old entries. The 76 entries include behavior maps from earlier today, yesterday, and the day before — all retained.\n\n2. **`cache-status` hides per-repo breakdown.** The aggregate \"Entries: 4 / Total size: 7.9 GB\" reports tells the user there's 7.9 GB of cache but doesn't reveal that 99.95% of it is in ONE repo. A user discovering the cache is large can't see which repo to target with `cache-clear`.\n\n3. **Coupled with INV-magul (cache-miss from .ops churn).** INV-magul documented that hypergumbo's cache shard key is polluted by tracker `.ops` file churn — meaning every tracker mutation invalidates the cache and forces a re-run. That high cache-miss rate × no-eviction = the 7.9 GB observed here. Fixing INV-magul would dramatically reduce growth rate; fixing R13-3 would bound the total size regardless.\n\n**Why this matters.**\n\n- **Disk exhaustion risk.** For a user with multiple active repos and AI-agent-driven workflows (~25 invocations/day across repos is plausible for active agent development), the cache can reach 50+ GB within a couple weeks before any warning surfaces.\n- **First-class CLI command (`cache-status`) gives misleading information.** \"4 entries, 7.9 GB\" sounds bounded; reality is \"one entry has 76 sub-entries averaging 104 MB each.\"\n- **Hand-clearing isn't ergonomic.** `cache-clear --older-than 1` clears everything older than 1 day across ALL repos. There's no per-repo or per-entry-count cap.\n\n**Severity.** Medium-high (disk-usage risk). The 7.9 GB cache happened on a single dev machine in 3 days; that's enough to fill an `/.cache` quota on shared systems.\n\n**Fix proposal — three changes.**\n\n1. **Auto-eviction policy.** Default: per-repo cache cap of, say, 10 entries or 1 GB (whichever is smaller). When a new entry is written, evict the oldest ones to stay under the cap. Configurable via env var or config file (e.g., `HYPERGUMBO_CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES_PER_REPO`, `HYPERGUMBO_CACHE_MAX_BYTES_PER_REPO`).\n\n2. **Make `cache-status` report per-repo breakdown.**\n   ```\n   Cache directory: ~/.cache/hypergumbo\n   Total size: 7.9 GB across 4 repos\n   \n   By repo:\n     473332f2ea511777   7.9 GB   76 entries   (last used: 22m ago)\n     9f18923999f7a303   104 KB    2 entries   (last used: 2d ago)\n     e9671acd244849c5    12 KB    1 entry     (last used: 2d ago)\n     tracker            148 KB   18 entries   (last used: 19h ago)\n   ```\n   Identifies the bloat source at a glance.\n\n3. **Improve `cache-clear` granularity.** Add `--repo REPO_ID` and `--keep-latest N` flags so a user can prune one repo's old entries without nuking the entire cache.\n\n**Test gap.** Add an integration test that simulates 20 `hypergumbo run` invocations with different fingerprints and asserts that the per-repo cache dir contains AT MOST the configured cap (e.g., 10) entries afterward.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/cli.py::cmd_cache_status` — current per-entry breakdown is missing\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/sketch_embeddings.py::_get_repo_state_hash` — produces the shard key (subject of INV-magul)\n- `INV-magul-...` (filed round 2) — root cause of high cache-miss rate (tracker .ops churn invalidates cache); fixing INV-magul reduces R13-3 growth rate but doesn't bound total size"  # 141f
    fields:  # 141f
      statement: "hypergumbo cache must have a bounded retention policy (per-repo entry count and/or total bytes cap, with automatic eviction of oldest entries on overflow). The cache-status subcommand must report per-repo breakdown so users can identify bloat sources."  # 141f
      root_cause: "cli.py::cmd_cache_status reports only aggregate stats. No code path evicts old cache entries. Combined with INV-magul (cache shard key churned by tracker .ops mutations), every run creates a new cache entry without cleaning up previous ones. On hypergumbo's own dev machine after 3 days of agent-driven workflow: 76 cache entries x ~104 MB each = 7.9 GB for ONE repo. Growth rate ~2.6 GB/day."  # 141f
